#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079402} :activity/id #uuid "b34e1933-3814-529f-88c0-52e2e04c4df5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391 :topic/id #uuid "9b93c13d-3e57-5a78-b8a6-0f0a4d64062e" :topic/math-academy-id 614} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079402 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079670 :tutorial/content "A second-order, linear, homogeneous ordinary differential equation with constant coefficients has the general form\n\n$$\na\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + b\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + cy =0,\n$$\n\nwhere $a, b,$ and $c$ are constants.\n\nTo find solutions, we can propose a trial solution of the form $y=e^{\\lambda x},$ where $\\lambda$ is a constant to be determined. Differentiating this gives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\lambda e^{\\lambda x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these into the general ODE, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a({\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x})+b(\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x})+c({e}^{\\lambda x}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}(a{\\lambda }^{2}+b\\lambda +c) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{\\lambda x}$ can never be zero, the expression in the parentheses must be zero:\n\n$$\na\\lambda^2+b\\lambda + c =0 .\n$$\n\nThis is called the **characteristic equation** (or **auxiliary equation**). The roots of this quadratic equation for $\\lambda$ give us the solutions to the original differential equation.\n\nNext, we'll use the characteristic equation to solve a specific example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c8c3fdb1-a293-55b7-bb3f-a3b91cd13c06" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4046 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "70588abd-28cf-57e8-91ad-d7a423e0a722" :step/math-academy-id 24219 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079553}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079403 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078494 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078337 :question/id #uuid "c47d5c55-80f0-5093-810b-bf9c718c5bb4" :question/math-academy-id "e-6587"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4bcb5277-6d80-5cea-9688-ddb98f434093" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049486}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265781} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357467} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421926}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing and Solving a Characteristic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "8acd33b3-bbd7-5f1f-ae06-547799b4f66d" :step/math-academy-id 24220 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079404}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079404 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078495 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078338 :question/id #uuid "2065790f-428d-5477-ac77-0c5d07853143" :question/math-academy-id "e-6588"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe7e5c4f-6ac0-58e8-8b95-fe5a2230341c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050522}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265830} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357470} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421928}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding General Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "3590f636-f3a1-5592-a64c-2fb248cb17db" :step/math-academy-id 24221 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079405}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079405 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078496 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078339 :question/id #uuid "1c7b0878-a386-5449-9ff4-c37c8cda1b92" :question/math-academy-id "e-6589"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fcb5d818-acb0-53c7-90f9-328256600175" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079160} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265832} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335737} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421927}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Non-Solutions of Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "4f98b762-c792-57e5-8e73-6917892dea16" :step/math-academy-id 24222} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079617 :tutorial/content "Let's solve the following differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}^2 y}{\\mathrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} x} + 6y =0\n$$\n\nFrom the previous slide, we know this ODE has a characteristic equation of the form $a\\lambda^2 + b\\lambda + c=0.$ Here, $a=1, b=5,$ and $c=6,$ so the characteristic equation is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe roots are $\\lambda_1 = -2$ and $\\lambda_2 = -3.$ Each root gives a particular solution to the ODE: $y_1=e^{-2x}$ and $y_2=e^{-3x}.$\n\nThe **general solution** is a linear combination of these particular solutions:\n\n$$\ny(x) = Ae^{-2x} + Be^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are arbitrary constants." :tutorial/id #uuid "fe48b08c-e63b-577c-ba34-bc61bc5b2966" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14884 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "88086b0a-3e17-59ee-bc49-6709f897fb53" :step/math-academy-id 607934 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079403}}] :activity/title "Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs: Characteristic Equations With Distinct Real Roots" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078644}]}
